#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557460 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x},\\,y=\\frac{8}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=4,\\,{x}^{2}y=7,\\,xy=4,\\,xy=8\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=4\\,⟹\\,u=4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=4\\,⟹\\,v=4 \\\\  & xy=8\\,⟹\\,v=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 7,\\,4\\le v\\le 8\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/47/475635e9575bd2f30069f2ee5f3b29bd6d7bf47db0cb6d5a96f981e36b8117c6.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{7}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{4}^{8}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)\\cdot (8-4) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557470 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{6}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=3,\\,{x}^{2}y=6,\\,xy=2,\\,xy=6\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=6\\,⟹\\,u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & xy=6\\,⟹\\,v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 6,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/a4/a42d50fe797c9450b27acde75ac7b69ed3e4ea5acc6c9dc93cd6df18b9ed1882.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)\\cdot (6-2) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557473 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{4}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=4,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 5,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/56/560d4d074d9d07eb034d92b8f1dfabfc3e59d659c91d80c3fedf68367a575e41.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5-4)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557489 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=3,\\,xy=7,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & xy=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/cf/cf598751d1bb1edda1bb65fd5b94d2ad5805289c48716f69a71394fd3684e920.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (7-3)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557627 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=- \\frac{5 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} - 6,\\,y=- \\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{t}{3} + 6,\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $- \\frac{103}{12}$ | {{field-1}} | $- \\frac{85}{4}$ | {{field-3}} | $- \\frac{527}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $\\frac{59}{12}$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | {{field-2}} | $- \\frac{22}{3}$ | $- \\frac{173}{12}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=- \\frac{5 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} - 6.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=- \\frac{5 (2)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 (2)}{3} - 6=(-6)+(-5)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{41}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{t}{3} + 6.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=- \\frac{3 (3)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{(3)}{3} + 6=(6)+\\left(- \\frac{27}{4}\\right)+(-1)=- \\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=- \\frac{5 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} - 6.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(4)=- \\frac{5 (4)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 (4)}{3} - 6=(-6)+(-20)+\\left(- \\frac{16}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{94}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $- \\frac{103}{12}$ | $- \\frac{41}{3}$ | $- \\frac{85}{4}$ | $- \\frac{94}{3}$ | $- \\frac{527}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $\\frac{59}{12}$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $- \\frac{7}{4}$ | $- \\frac{22}{3}$ | $- \\frac{173}{12}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557631 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2 t}{3} + 4,\\,y=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{4 t}{3} - 4,\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $4$ | $\\frac{65}{12}$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{51}{4}$ | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{313}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $- \\frac{29}{12}$ | $- \\frac{1}{3}$ | {{field-2}} | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{107}{12}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2 t}{3} + 4.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=\\frac{3 (2)^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2 (2)}{3} + 4=(4)+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+(3)=\\frac{25}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{4 t}{3} - 4.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=\\frac{(3)^{2}}{4} + \\frac{4 (3)}{3} - 4=(-4)+\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)+(4)=\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2 t}{3} + 4.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(4)=\\frac{3 (4)^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2 (4)}{3} + 4=(4)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)+(12)=\\frac{56}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $4$ | $\\frac{65}{12}$ | $\\frac{25}{3}$ | $\\frac{51}{4}$ | $\\frac{56}{3}$ | $\\frac{313}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $- \\frac{29}{12}$ | $- \\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{107}{12}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557649 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^{3}}{4} + \\frac{t^{2}}{3} - 6,\\,y=- \\frac{t^{3}}{4} + \\frac{2 t^{2}}{3} - 3,\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $- \\frac{65}{12}$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{403}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $- \\frac{31}{12}$ | $- \\frac{7}{3}$ | {{field-2}} | $- \\frac{25}{3}$ | $- \\frac{211}{12}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{t^{3}}{4} + \\frac{t^{2}}{3} - 6.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=\\frac{(2)^{3}}{4} + \\frac{(2)^{2}}{3} - 6=(-6)+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+(2)=- \\frac{8}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{t^{3}}{4} + \\frac{2 t^{2}}{3} - 3.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=- \\frac{(3)^{3}}{4} + \\frac{2 (3)^{2}}{3} - 3=(-3)+\\left(- \\frac{27}{4}\\right)+(6)=- \\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{t^{3}}{4} + \\frac{t^{2}}{3} - 6.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(4)=\\frac{(4)^{3}}{4} + \\frac{(4)^{2}}{3} - 6=(-6)+\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)+(16)=\\frac{46}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $- \\frac{65}{12}$ | $- \\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{46}{3}$ | $\\frac{403}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $- \\frac{31}{12}$ | $- \\frac{7}{3}$ | $- \\frac{15}{4}$ | $- \\frac{25}{3}$ | $- \\frac{211}{12}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557664 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{t}{3} - 6,\\,y=- \\frac{t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2 t}{3} - 3,\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $- \\frac{65}{12}$ | {{field-1}} | $- \\frac{11}{4}$ | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{23}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $- \\frac{31}{12}$ | $- \\frac{8}{3}$ | {{field-2}} | $- \\frac{13}{3}$ | $- \\frac{71}{12}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{t}{3} - 6.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4} + \\frac{(2)}{3} - 6=(-6)+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+(1)=- \\frac{13}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2 t}{3} - 3.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=- \\frac{(3)^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2 (3)}{3} - 3=(-3)+\\left(- \\frac{9}{4}\\right)+(2)=- \\frac{13}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $x=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{t}{3} - 6.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(4)=\\frac{(4)^{2}}{4} + \\frac{(4)}{3} - 6=(-6)+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+(4)=- \\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $- \\frac{65}{12}$ | $- \\frac{13}{3}$ | $- \\frac{11}{4}$ | $- \\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{23}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $- \\frac{31}{12}$ | $- \\frac{8}{3}$ | $- \\frac{13}{4}$ | $- \\frac{13}{3}$ | $- \\frac{71