t(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{20}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{5 t^{2}}{4} + \\frac{4 t}{3} + 2.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=\\frac{5 (3)^{2}}{4} + \\frac{4 (3)}{3} + 2=(2)+(4)+\\left(\\frac{45}{4}\\right)=\\frac{69}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=- \\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{t}{3} - 3.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(4)=- \\frac{3 (4)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{(4)}{3} - 3=(-3)+(-12)+\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{49}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $- \\frac{49}{12}$ | $- \\frac{20}{3}$ | $- \\frac{43}{4}$ | $- \\frac{49}{3}$ | $- \\frac{281}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $\\frac{55}{12}$ | $\\frac{29}{3}$ | $\\frac{69}{4}$ | $\\frac{82}{3}$ | $\\frac{479}{12}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557691 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=- \\frac{5 t^{3}}{4} - \\frac{4 t^{2}}{3} - 6,\\,y=- \\frac{3 t^{3}}{4} - \\frac{t^{2}}{3} + 6,\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $- \\frac{103}{12}$ | {{field-1}} | $- \\frac{207}{4}$ | {{field-3}} | $- \\frac{2347}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $\\frac{59}{12}$ | $- \\frac{4}{3}$ | {{field-2}} | $- \\frac{142}{3}$ | $- \\frac{1153}{12}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=- \\frac{5 t^{3}}{4} - \\frac{4 t^{2}}{3} - 6.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=- \\frac{5 (2)^{3}}{4} - \\frac{4 (2)^{2}}{3} - 6=(-6)+(-10)+\\left(- \\frac{16}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{64}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{3 t^{3}}{4} - \\frac{t^{2}}{3} + 6.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=- \\frac{3 (3)^{3}}{4} - \\frac{(3)^{2}}{3} + 6=(6)+\\left(- \\frac{81}{4}\\right)+(-3)=- \\frac{69}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $$\n\\displaystyle x=- \\frac{5 t^{3}}{4} - \\frac{4 t^{2}}{3} - 6.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(4)=- \\frac{5 (4)^{3}}{4} - \\frac{4 (4)^{2}}{3} - 6=(-6)+(-80)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{322}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $- \\frac{103}{12}$ | $- \\frac{64}{3}$ | $- \\frac{207}{4}$ | $- \\frac{322}{3}$ | $- \\frac{2347}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $\\frac{59}{12}$ | $- \\frac{4}{3}$ | $- \\frac{69}{4}$ | $- \\frac{142}{3}$ | $- \\frac{1153}{12}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557694 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} + 2,\\,y=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{2 t}{3} + 3,\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{19}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{37}{12}$ | $\\frac{14}{3}$ | {{field-2}} | $\\frac{37}{3}$ | $\\frac{221}{12}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} + 2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 (2)}{3} + 2=(2)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right)+(1)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{2 t}{3} + 3.\n$$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=\\frac{3 (3)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{2 (3)}{3} + 3=(3)+(-2)+\\left(\\frac{27}{4}\\right)=\\frac{31}{4}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $x=\\frac{t^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 t}{3} + 2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(4)=\\frac{(4)^{2}}{4} - \\frac{4 (4)}{3} + 2=(2)+\\left(- \\frac{16}{3}\\right)+(4)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $2$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{19}{12}$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{37}{12}$ | $\\frac{14}{3}$ | $\\frac{31}{4}$ | $\\frac{37}{3}$ | $\\frac{221}{12}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557800 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(4 x^{2} y - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(3 x y^{2} + 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + 16 y^{2}=144$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(4,0)$ to the point $(-4,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\pi].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(4 x^{2} y - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(3 x y^{2} + 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(4 x^{2} y - 2 y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(3 x y^{2} + 2 x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(\\left(4 x^{2} y - 2 y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(3 x y^{2} + 2 x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(\\left(192 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(108 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(\\left(- 768 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(24 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(324 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(- 444 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 24\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(\\frac{111 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{63}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[- \\frac{63 t}{2} + \\frac{111 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{8}\\right]_{0}^{\\pi}\\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{63 \\left(\\pi\\right)}{2} + \\frac{111 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}}{8}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{63 \\left(0\\right)}{2} + \\frac{111 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{8}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{63 \\pi}{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\frac{63 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557802 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(3 x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(3 x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(3 x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(3 x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(108 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(117 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{93}{8} - \\frac{117 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[\\frac{93 t}{8} - \\frac{117 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{32}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{93 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{8} - \\frac{117 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{32}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{93 \\left(0\\right)}{8} - \\frac{117 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{32}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(\\frac{93 \\pi}{16}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{93 \\pi}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557820 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- x y\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- x y\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 27 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(81 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(78 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557824 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 3 x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $16 x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=144$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $4,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 3 x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- 3 x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- 3 x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 144 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 144 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(432 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 576 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 144 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(18 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 30\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[- 30 t + \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(- 30 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)-\\left(- 30 \\left(0\\right) + \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- 15 \\pi\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 15 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557830 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x y^{2} - 4 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=36$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x y^{2} - 4 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- 2 x y^{2} - 4 x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- 2 x y^{2} - 4 x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 36 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(48 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 24 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 108 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 60 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 24\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{15 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{63}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[- \\frac{63 t}{2} + \\frac{15 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{8}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{63 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2} + \\frac{15 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{8}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{63 \\left(0\\right)}{2} + \\frac{15 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{8}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{63 \\pi}{4}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\frac{63 \\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557836 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(51 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{27}{8} - \\frac{51 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[\\frac{27 t}{8} - \\frac{51 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{32}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{27 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{8} - \\frac{51 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{32}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{27 \\left(0\\right)}{8} - \\frac{51 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{32}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(\\frac{27 \\pi}{16}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{27 \\pi}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557837 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(39 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{39 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[- 3 t + \\frac{39 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(- 3 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + \\frac{39 \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\left(0\\right) + \\frac{39 \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{39}{2} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558573 :question/problem "Consider the following system  of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=3 \\\\ x-y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(x,y)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ into $x-y=1,$ then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-y & =1 \\\\ -y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558773 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 1\\le x + y\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le x - y\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le z\\le \\left(x - y\\right) \\left(x + y\\right)\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + y,\\quad v=x - y,\\quad w=\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nWrite the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4 \\sqrt{z}\\,dV\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + y,\\quad v=x - y,\\quad w=\\sqrt{z}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 1\\le u\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le w\\le \\sqrt{u v}\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0\\\\1 & -1 & 0\\\\0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}-(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ Since $z\\ge1$ and $w=\\sqrt z$, the branch is $w>0$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4 \\sqrt{z}\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(4 w\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(4 w\\right)\\left|- w\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(4 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558782 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 1\\le x y\\le 2,\\quad -2\\le y\\le -1,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x y,\\quad v=y,\\quad w=4 z,\n$$\n\nWrite the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 3 x y^{2} + y z\\,dV\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x y,\\quad v=y,\\quad w=4 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 1\\le u\\le 2,\\quad -2\\le v\\le -1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 8\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0\\\\0 & 1 & 0\\\\0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(y)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(x)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0\\\\0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=4 y\\\\ &=4 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4 v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ The sign of $v$ is fixed throughout $Δ$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 3 x y^{2} + y z\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(\\frac{v \\left(12 u + w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(\\frac{v \\left(12 u + w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\left|\\frac{1}{4 v}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{8}\\left(- \\frac{3 u}{4} - \\frac{w}{16}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558793 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 1\\le x + y\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le x - y\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le z\\le \\left(x - y\\right) \\left(x + y\\right)\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + y,\\quad v=x - y,\\quad w=\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nWrite the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 3 \\sqrt{z}\\,dV\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + y,\\quad v=x - y,\\quad w=\\sqrt{z}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le w\\le \\sqrt{u v}\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0\\\\1 & -1 & 0\\\\0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}-(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ Since $z\\ge1$ and $w=\\sqrt z$, the branch is $w>0$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 3 \\sqrt{z}\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 w\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 w\\right)\\left|- w\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(3 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558801 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 0\\le x + 3 y\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\le - 3 y + z\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\le z\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + 3 y,\\quad v=- 3 y + z,\\quad w=z,\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(x + z \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 y - z \\right)}\\,dV=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}\\left(\\boxed{?}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + 3 y,\\quad v=- 3 y + z,\\quad w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\le w\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0\\\\0 & -3 & 1\\\\0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(1)\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1\\\\0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=-3\\\\ &=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(x + z \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 y - z \\right)}\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}\\right)\\left|- \\frac{1}{3}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}\\left(\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558802 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 1\\le x y\\le 2,\\quad 1\\le y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x y,\\quad v=y,\\quad w=4 z,\n$$\n\nWrite the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 2 x y^{2} + 3 y z\\,dV\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x y,\\quad v=y,\\quad w=4 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 1\\le u\\le 2,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 12\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0\\\\0 & 1 & 0\\\\0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(y)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(x)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0\\\\0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=4 y\\\\ &=4 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4 v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ The sign of $v$ is fixed throughout $Δ$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2 x y^{2} + 3 y z\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(\\frac{v \\left(8 u + 3 w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(\\frac{v \\left(8 u + 3 w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\left|\\frac{1}{4 v}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{12}\\left(\\frac{u}{2} + \\frac{3 w}{16}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558807 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\le 2 x + y\\le \\pi,\\quad 0\\le y + z\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad -1\\le z\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + z,\\quad w=z,\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 3 \\sin{\\left(2 x + y \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y + z \\right)}\\,dV=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{-1}^{5}\\left(\\boxed{?}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + z,\\quad w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\le u\\le \\pi,\\quad 0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad -1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0\\\\0 & 1 & 1\\\\0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1\\\\0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=2\\\\ &=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 3 \\sin{\\left(2 x + y \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y + z \\right)}\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left|\\frac{1}{2}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{-1}^{5}\\left(\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558813 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t + 2,\\,y=t^{2} - 4,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1(-1)+2=1, \\\\ y & =1(-1)^2-4=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\quad y\\prime(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{2 t}{1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(-1)}{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-3) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(1)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558821 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t + 4,\\,y=t^{2} + 4,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(1)+4=8, \\\\ y & =1(1)^2+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(8,5).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\quad y\\prime(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{2 t}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(1)}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(5) & =-2(x-(8)) \\\\ y & =21 - 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558835 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t + 3,\\,y=4 t^{2} + 2,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1(1)+3=4, \\\\ y & =4(1)^2+2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(4,6).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\quad y\\prime(t)=8 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{8 t}{1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8(1)}{1}=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(8)}=- \\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(6) & =- \\frac{1}{8}(x-(4)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{13}{2} - \\frac{x}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558836 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t - 2,\\,y=4 t^{2} - 3,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(1)-2=2, \\\\ y & =4(1)^2-3=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\quad y\\prime(t)=8 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{8 t}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8(1)}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(2)}=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(1) & =- \\frac{1}{2}(x-(2)) \\\\ y & =2 - \\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558845 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t+5,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2}+3,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Give exact answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)+5=-2+5 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})+3=3+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(3,6).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,6)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}((x-5)-(-2)) \\\\ (y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}((x-5)+2) \\\\ (y-3)-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)-1 \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)-1+3 \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =- \\frac{1}{2}(0-(3))+6 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{15}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558847 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3 t + 1,\\,y=2 t^{2} + 2,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3(-1)+1=-2, \\\\ y & =2(-1)^2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,4).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3,\\quad y\\prime(t)=4 t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y\\prime(t)}{x\\prime(t)}=\\frac{4 t}{3}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{4(-1)}{3}=- \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(4) & =\\frac{3}{4}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558856 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558865 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=9.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,9).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18c4c61a55dd2a361b82a208eb9a0d26f3fcfa213fe83b05f23644c9f79becaa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{9}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}[v]_{u^{2}}^{9}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}(9)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}(9 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 9 u]_{-3}^{3} \\\\ &=1(((27)+(-9))-((-27)+(9))) \\\\ &=1(36) \\\\ &=36\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558868 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=36.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 4 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=36.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=36\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 6,36).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-6\\le u\\le 6,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 36\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/13/132cec4e3586ef176e2c94e05bbe8860063cce2cef4d0835159da844ea43b8ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-4\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{36}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[v]_{u^{2}}^{36}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 36 u]_{-6}^{6} \\\\ &=1(((216)+(-72))-((-216)+(72))) \\\\ &=1(288) \\\\ &=288\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558872 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558873 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=36.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $8 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=36.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=36\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 6,36).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-6\\le u\\le 6,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 36\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/13/132cec4e3586ef176e2c94e05bbe8860063cce2cef4d0835159da844ea43b8ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}8&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(10)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{10}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-6}^{6}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{36}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-6}^{6}[v]_{u^{2}}^{36}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-6}^{6}(36)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-6}^{6}(36 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 36 u]_{-6}^{6} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(((216)+(-72))-((-216)+(72))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(288) \\\\ &=\\frac{144}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558881 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=25.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $5 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=25.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 5,25).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 25\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca54518b097929ecf8756bc699c5fef32de07b174134781e778fdcc395e964fe.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}5&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(7)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{7}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-5}^{5}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{25}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-5}^{5}[v]_{u^{2}}^{25}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-5}^{5}(25)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-5}^{5}(25 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 25 u]_{-5}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}(((125)+\\left(- \\frac{125}{3}\\right))-((-125)+\\left(\\frac{125}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{500}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{500}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
